you learn and accompany with it this was  Angela Irizarry before her surgery she  didn't have much energy and couldn't do  the things that three-year-old children  enjoy like run or ride a bike very far  Angela was born with a congenital heart  defect that left her with one ventricle  pumping blood through her body instead  of to one of the most severe forms of  congenital heart disease are a diverse  group of structural anomalies that  result in what's called single ventricle  physiology these are children that are  basically born with half a heart this is  Angela three months after the  groundbreaking operation in August 2011  to graft a new blood vessel made of her  body's own cells to help her heart  function efficiently it was the first  time this operation had been performed  in the United States  it made Angela's heart complete before  when she wants to run with her brother  she could tire is here and now she could  she doesn't want to stop T can keep  going right yes  so she's her oxygen and Lego is very  very good now the Irizarry family's long  journey began in November 2007 when  Angela was born with only one functional  ventricle the hearts to ventricles  collect in pump blood through the body  one sends blood to the lungs where it is  mixed with oxygen the second pumps that  oxygenated blood back to the body's  circulatory system with one ventricle  trying to do the work of two there is  much less oxygen flowing to the body  these children are typically known as  blue babies because they're very  cyanotic because their oxygenated blood  and deoxygenated  is mixed on and so these this this  results in in blue discoloration without  surgical intervention there is a 70%  mortality in the first year of life and  survival to adulthood is very unusual an  order for the surgeon to successfully  rearrange the plumbing it requires the  use of synthetic or man-made materials  specifically requires use of a vascular  graft or conduit and it turns out that  complications arising from the use of  these synthetic materials are a leading  cause of post-operative morbidity and  mortality the synthetic grafts are  susceptible to clotting infection and  rejection all life-threatening  furthermore they can't grow with the  child who must endure repeated  open-heart surgeries to replace the  graft so children can actually outgrow  their operations just the way they can  outgrow their shoes but imagine living  in a world where every time your child  outgrew their shoes they needed to go  back to the operating room and have an  operation dr. Christopher Breuer is a  pediatric surgeon at the Yale School of  Medicine he came to Yale in 2003  determined to make a difference on a  daily basis during my training I would  see problems arise for which we had no  real solutions and because we couldn't  fix these problems I would watch  children die and watch families fall  apart working with fellow Yale cardiac  surgeon Toshi chanooka who had developed  a version of this surgery in Japan and a  team of biomedical engineers from Yale  led by Marc Saltzman the team came up  with a novel idea what if they were able  to construct a vascular graft from the  patient's own cells their hypothesis was  that it would grow with the child and  would not be rejected so the the graft  is made from a fabric of a degradable  polymer the polymers called poly lactic  glycolic acid and it's a polymer that's  well known to be safe in in people it's  been used in medicine for for many many  decades and what was unique here is that  it's it's fashioned into a tube so it  takes the form of a graft and then it's  seeded with cells from the patient  and those are collected from the bone  marrow and and then placed in contact  with this graft and then incubated for a  short period of time before the surgeons  implant it into the into the site  what makes this better than a  conventional synthetic graft is is  really twofold one you're using cells  from the patient and placing them on  this material and what develops then is  a real tissue that's formed from the  patient's own cells so there's no  immunological problems the synthetic  material itself disappears and so  there's nothing left behind to create  any toxic effects so within a few months  after the operation what's left is none  of the polymer and in its place is a  normal blood vessel we set out trying to  create a tissue engineered vascular  graft that we could use instead of using  these synthetic man-made grafts with the  hope that if we could create these from  your own tissues on that they would be  living vascular conduits and would not  have all these problems they'd have a  lower incidence of thromboembolic  complications they'd be less susceptible  to infection and they'd have growth  potential and so far our hypothesis is  proven correct actually these these  grafts do work quite well and do possess  growth potential  you know precluding the need for  additional surgeries in these children  in other words they had naturally  rebuilt a defective heart to make it  complete Breuer and his team believed  they had developed a successful  procedure but convincing the US Food and  Drug Administration that this was safe  and effective took time and patience was  well worth the effort the FDA was very  good to work with  we were both focused on one thing we  wanted to make this trial as safe as  possible and the FDA had  a number of really excellent suggestions  that helped us design a better and safer  study Chris Brewer even let his hair and  beard grow long vowing not to cut them  until FDA approval came in November 2009  it finally did it was terrifying and  exciting at the same time the next task  was finding the first patient Angela  Irizarry and her family came to see dr.  brewer to be evaluated you know we  talked to a number of people but really  felt that the Irizarry family were quite  a special family they were all very  bright and very intelligent they asked  very probing and inappropriate questions  and so it seemed like the right  combination we had the child who would  potentially benefit from this procedure  who met all of my criteria to be  included in the study and we had a  family that understood what this was all  about and you know really understood the  potential risks involved and the  potential benefits and so I felt like I  could really obtain what's called  informed consent what was it like for  you when Angela was sick what were you  feeling  well I'm believing God and I have strong  faith you forgot  send me the baby wait if he sent me the  baby he's gonna send me the Angels took  her her so when we first met Angela um  you know she did have some cyanosis her  her her energy level wasn't really what  you'd expect it to be for for a little  three-year-old so the thought of being  able to perform an operation and have a  child improve improve their oxygenation  level improved their energy level and  have better growth and development you  know was what we were looking for you  know she has her condition but anyway  she's been very healthy you know so I  was I was believed that everything is  gonna be okay I'm trust so much to the  help of the Yale Hospital  Angela's surgery was scheduled for  August 2011 we probably did about 20  practice runs just to get ready for this  and my my lab is probably more like a  like a drag practice than a laboratory  at times where we actually had stop  watches and we were you know trying to  save every second we we could  Angela's surgical team was led by  doctors Breuer and Jean oka and  cardiothoracic surgeon Gary cuff on the  day of the procedure we actually had  things scripted out with times and and  it was you know probably sort of like  managing a play or a television show and  that we had to make sure we could you  know meeting our milestones and  accomplishing things at the same time  and it actually worked out perfectly  within 30 seconds of the surgeon needing  the graft it was they're ready and had  met all of its release criteria so that  the hard work paid off as she recovered  angela was attentively cared for by  doctors and nurses at the Yale New Haven  Children's Hospital she had one  complication an infection which was  successfully treated but kept her in the  hospital for two weeks instead of one  after that  Angela came home to complete her  recovery slowly she began to do things  she couldn't do before before she was  normal but you need to stop limit her  physical activities now she doesn't have  an agreement in November  Angela celebrated her fourth birthday at  home a healthy little girl who showed  all signs of being able to live a normal  life her improvement was really  remarkable the last time I saw her in  clinic come she really was a normal  three-year-old running around and her  mom informed me that for the first time  she can ride a bicycle what about the  future Angela can't go to school for  another year because her immune system  is not completely up to snuff yet but  dr. brewer is hopeful that her body will  adapt yeah I think at the end of the day  I think Angela will will have be a very  normal child you know she's a bright  little girl and with the surgery now I  think her heart function is much  improved and and you know I'd anticipate  that she'll be back in school and  you know graduated from high school and  going on to things beyond my hope for  her is well I think that that the same  for all moms that they can they can do  whatever they want they can grow up and  be good persons and I will love that she  can do you know be a doctor or something  that she can help kids the same way that  they help her for Chris Brewer who  devoted so much of his life to this  mission to save lives  there is great satisfaction so you saved  one little girl's life and you've opened  the way for other people to live longer  better more productive lives how does  that make you feel that's about as good  as it gets  you
 

